course: dictionary
lesson: 7739
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Pulse» is one of them. Its meaning depends on the sentence around it, so the context tells you which meaning to choose. Read the words before and after the tricky word and ask: which meaning fits here?
explain: «Her pulse raced after the playground race.» Here pulse is the beat of her heart, which you can feel on your wrist or neck. In a different sentence, «Lentils and peas are pulses», pulse means a seed you can eat. Same spelling, different thing.
explain: Some words are also used as verbs, which are action words. «The lamp pulsed with light» means the light beat or throbbed in a steady rhythm. To find the right meaning, swap your idea into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a multiple-meaning word.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word, and they show you which meaning is right.
fact: «Pulse» means the beat of your heart that you can feel on your wrist or neck.
fact: «Pulse» can also mean one short beat, burst of light, sound or energy, or a seed you can eat, like lentils, beans and peas.
fact: «Pulse» as a verb means to beat or throb in a steady rhythm, as in «pulsed» and «pulsing».
exercise: Mia counts her «pulse» on her wrist after the race. What is her pulse? | A bean in a pot | *The beat of her heart that she can feel | A loud sound from the playground | A short burst of light
exercise: «Pulses are rich in protein, so they are good for you.» What are pulses in this sentence? | Beats of your heart | Bursts of light in the sky | *Seeds you can eat, like lentils, beans and peas | Sounds from a drum
exercise: «The neon sign pulsed red, then went dark, then pulsed again.» What does pulsed mean? | Fell to the ground | Was cooked and eaten | *Beat or throb in a steady rhythm | Stayed still and quiet
test: A child's «resting pulse» is often about how many beats a minute? | 170 to 190 | *70 to 100 | 7 to 10 | 700 to 1000
test: Galileo timed a swinging lamp «with his pulse». What did he do? | Made a loud sound with a drum | Ate some lentils while he watched | Shone a light on the lamp | *Counted his heartbeats to time the swings
exam: To work out your pulse, you count the beats for 15 seconds and multiply by 4; what does the answer tell you? | How far you ran | Your height in centimetres | *Your heartbeats per minute | How many seeds you ate
